  "step_2": "The individual first examined the outcomes of both lotteries. They noted that both lotteries have the same expected value of -5€. However, they emphasized that the decision is made in a single play, and in that context, the 0.1% chance of losing 5000€ in Lottery A is 'extremely unlikely'. In contrast, Lottery B guarantees a 5€ loss. The individual expresses aversion to the 'certainty of loss' in B and chooses A to avoid this guaranteed loss, even though A involves a very small risk of a large loss. The justification centers on preferring a small chance of a large loss over a sure small loss, particularly due to the one-shot nature of the decision and the perceived unlikelihood of the bad outcome in A.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on treating small probability differences as negligible and then selecting the lottery with the more favorable maximum outcome. In this case, the individual does treat the 0.1% probability as negligible (calling it 'extremely unlikely'), and they favor Lottery A because it offers a chance (99.9%) of losing nothing, which is better than B's certain -5€. Their focus on the maximum outcome (0€ vs -5€) and dismissal of the small probability as effectively irrelevant aligns with the reason’s rule. Although the individual frames their choice in terms of avoiding a certain loss, this is functionally equivalent to preferring the more favorable maximum outcome when probabilities are considered negligible. The information considered (probabilities as negligible, focus on best possible outcome) and the justification (choosing A due to better outcome in the likely case) match the decision reason closely.",
  "final_assessment_score": 95
